## The standard specifies minimums and an ordering, not sizes

Three of the four are guaranteed. The one that is not — `sizeof(int) == 4` — is true on virtually every machine you will use, and that is precisely what makes it dangerous.

## What is actually promised

```
sizeof(char) == 1                               always, by definition
1 == sizeof(char) <= sizeof(short) <= sizeof(int)
     <= sizeof(long) <= sizeof(long long)
```

with minimum widths in bits:

| Type | At least | Typical on x86-64 Linux |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `char` | 8 | 8 |
| `short` | 16 | 16 |
| `int` | **16** | 32 |
| `long` | 32 | **64** on Linux, **32** on Windows |
| `long long` | 64 | 64 |

Look at the `long` row. It is 8 bytes on 64-bit Linux and 4 bytes on 64-bit Windows, both perfectly conforming. Code that assumes `long` is 64 bits is not portable between two operating systems on the *same processor* — this is the LP64 against LLP64 split, and it is the commonest real-world size bug there is.

## What to do about it

**When the width matters, say so.** From `<cstdint>`:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>

std::int32_t  exactly_32_bits{};       // exactly, or does not compile
std::int64_t  exactly_64_bits{};
std::uint8_t  a_byte{};
std::int_fast32_t at_least_32_and_quick{};
```

These are the right types for a file format, a network protocol, or anything where a number has to be a specific number of bits. Your notes cover them, and this is what they are for.

**When it does not matter, use `int`.** It is the type the implementation considers natural, and reaching for `std::int32_t` everywhere is noise.

**For a size or an index, use `std::size_t`** — that is what it means, and it is what the standard library's `.size()` returns.

## The unsigned part of `size_t` bites

```cpp
std::cout << sizeof(int) - sizeof(double) << '\n';
```

This does not print -4. Both operands are `std::size_t`, which is unsigned, so the subtraction wraps: on a 64-bit machine it prints **18446744073709551612**.

This is the same trap as comparing a signed index against `.size()`, and it is why `-Wsign-compare` exists. The habit worth forming: **never subtract two sizes**, and if you must, cast to a signed type first and be explicit about it.

## Finding out what your machine does

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <climits>

int main()
{
    std::cout << "char      " << sizeof(char)      << '\n';
    std::cout << "short     " << sizeof(short)     << '\n';
    std::cout << "int       " << sizeof(int)       << '\n';
    std::cout << "long      " << sizeof(long)      << '\n';
    std::cout << "long long " << sizeof(long long) << '\n';
    std::cout << "CHAR_BIT  " << CHAR_BIT          << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

Worth running once, and worth running again in Compiler Explorer with the target set to something other than x86-64 — the numbers change, and seeing them change is more convincing than reading that they can.
